@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Storage design";
}

<div class="page-head">
    <div>
        <div class="eyebrow">Design Rationale</div>
        <h1>How storage is laid out</h1>
        <p class="lede">
            The decisions behind the schema, and why each one suits the way ABC Retail
            actually reads and writes this data.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="panel">
    <div class="panel-body prose">
        <h2>Why Table Storage</h2>
        <p>
            ABC Retail's relational database struggled under seasonal load because every read
            competed with every write on the same server. Table Storage removes that ceiling by
            partitioning data across storage nodes, and it charges per gigabyte rather than per
            provisioned instance. The trade is a narrower query surface: there are no joins, no
            aggregate functions and no secondary indexes, so the key design has to carry the weight
            that indexes would carry elsewhere.
        </p>

        <h2>CustomerProfiles</h2>
        <p>
            Every customer sits in one partition, <code>CUSTOMER</code>, with a generated GUID as
            the row key. At profile volumes a single partition is comfortable, and keeping the table
            that way means any group of customers can still be written in one entity group
            transaction, since those are scoped to a partition. If the customer base outgrew a single
            partition server, region would be the natural key to split on, because fulfilment queries
            are already regional.
        </p>
        <p>
            Search is applied after the read rather than in the query, because Table Storage has no
            contains operator. That is honest at this scale and would be replaced by a search index
            at a larger one.
        </p>

        <h2>Products</h2>
        <p>
            Category is the partition key. The catalogue is browsed one category at a time far more
            often than it is read whole, so this turns the dominant query into a single-partition
            scan instead of a full table scan, and it spreads writes across partition servers during
            a bulk catalogue load. The cost is that moving a product between categories changes its
            identity, which is why the edit path performs a delete and an insert rather than an
            update in place.
        </p>
        <p>
            Prices are stored as <code>Double</code>. Table Storage has no decimal type, so anything
            performing real money arithmetic would convert on read rather than accumulate in
            floating point.
        </p>

        <h2>Blob Storage for imagery</h2>
        <p>
            Images never enter the table. Table Storage caps an entity at one megabyte and a property
            at sixty-four kilobytes, so imagery belongs in Blob Storage regardless. The table holds
            the blob URL and the blob name, the first so a view can point an image tag straight at it,
            the second so the blob can be replaced or removed when the product changes.
        </p>
        <p>
            The container grants anonymous read at blob level. That lets the browser fetch each image
            directly from the storage account instead of routing bytes through the web application,
            which keeps thumbnails off the App Service compute budget entirely. Shared access
            signatures would be the alternative where the imagery was not already public, at the cost
            of a signing round trip per image.
        </p>

        <h2>Ordering of writes</h2>
        <p>
            When a product is created the blob is uploaded before the table entity is written, and
            when one is deleted the blob is removed before the entity. Failing partway through leaves
            an unreferenced blob rather than a catalogue entry pointing at an image that does not
            exist, which is the cheaper of the two failures to clean up.
        </p>

        <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
        <p>
            The storage services sit behind interfaces resolved through dependency injection, so the
            queue and file share work that follows is additive. Neither the controllers nor the views
            hold a reference to an Azure SDK type.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>
